Colour the sidebar scroll bars of the browser from QtCurve

The static theme carries the two sidebar colours, so the browser marks
the root of each sidebar document. Its own rules then force a grey pair
of scroll bar colours on that root. The sidebars therefore kept a grey
bar while every other surface took the colours of QtCurve.

The owned sheet now carries one rule of that root. It states the thumb
and the track as literal colours under !important. The sheet stands at
the user origin and the rules of the browser carry no !important, so the
rule wins. buildSheet() writes each row of the map as one custom
property, and a scroll bar colour is no custom property. The rule
therefore takes a section of its own, beside the images of the tool box
and the shadow of a popup.

The pair reads the helper of palette.cpp that the block of the GTK sheet
reads, so the two arms of the theme hold one derivation. Two new roles
of ffmap.h carry the pair, and the rows of the map hold neither one. The
pin of the properties holds names of the browser alone, and the schema
of the static theme names no scroll bar. A user of the static theme
alone therefore keeps the grey pair.

The rule paints the sidebars of HTML. The classic tree of XUL reads no
scroll bar colour of CSS, and the block of the GTK sheet colours that
tree.

assert-ff-scrollbar.sh binds the rule to the settings over eleven arms.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 10 of 13. The branch holds those thirteen commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme. Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve
holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

